      "text": " A) True.\n\nWait, but I'm getting confused again.Let me try to think differently.\n\nThe statement in question is: If George will not compose, then George cannot compose.\n\nIn logical terms: \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acCan.\n\nWe need to see if this is necessarily true, false, or uncertain.\n\nFrom the premises, we have:\n\n3. (Can \u2227 Want) \u2192 Will.\n\nThe contrapositive is \u00acWill \u2192 \u00ac(Can \u2227 Want), which is \u00acWill \u2192 (\u00acCan \u2228 \u00acWant).\n\nSo, if George won't compose, then either he can't compose or he doesn't want to compose.\n\nThe statement in question is \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acCan, which is a stronger statement than the contrapositive because it only considers \u00acCan, ignoring the possibility of \u00acWant.\n\nTherefore, the statement in question is not necessarily true because it's possible that George can compose (Can is true) but doesn't want to (Want is false), leading to \u00acWill, but \u00acCan is false.\n\nHence, the statement is false because it's not always true.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought it was true, but now I'm thinking it's false.\n\nLet me clarify:\n\nThe statement in question is: If George will not compose, then he cannot compose.\n\nFrom the contrapositive of premise 3, we have that if George will not compose, then either he cannot compose or he does not want to compose.\n\nSo, the statement in question is saying that if he won't compose, then he cannot compose. But the contrapositive allows for the possibility that he can compose but doesn't want to, which would mean that the statement in question is not necessarily true because there's a scenario where he can compose but won't.\n\nTherefore, the statement is false because it's not always true.\n\nWait, but I'm getting conflicting conclusions here. Let me try to structure it more clearly.\n\nPremise 3: (Can \u2227 Want) \u2192 Will.\n\nContrapositive: \u00acWill \u2192 \u00ac(Can \u2227 Want) \u2192 \u00acCan \u2228 \u00acWant.\n\nSo, if George won't compose, then either he can't compose or he doesn't want to.\n\nThe statement in question is: \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acCan.\n\nThis is equivalent to saying that if George won't compose, then he can't compose.\n\nBut from the contrapositive, we know that it's possible that George can compose (Can is true) but doesn't want to (Want is false), which would make \u00acWill true, but \u00acCan would be false.\n\nTherefore, the statement in question is not necessarily true because there's a scenario where George can compose but won't, making the statement false.\n\nHence, the statement is false.\n\nWait, but I'm getting confused again. Let me try to think of it in terms of logical equivalence.\n\nThe statement in question is \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acCan.\n\nThe contrapositive of this is Can \u2192 Will.\n\nBut from premise 3, we have (Can \u2227 Want) \u2192 Will.\n\nSo, Can alone doesn't necessarily lead to Will; it's Can and Want together.\n\nTherefore, Can \u2192 Will is not necessarily true because Want might be false.\n\nHence, the contrapositive of the statement in question (Can \u2192 Will) is not necessarily true, so the statement in question (\u00acWill \u2192 \u00acCan) is not necessarily true.\n\nTherefore, the statement is false because it's not always true.\n\nWait, but I'm not sure. Let me think of it in terms of possible worlds.\n\nCase 1: George can compose (Can=true), wants to compose (Want=true). Then, from premise 3, he will compose (Will=true). So, \u00acWill is false, so the statement in question is trivially true.\n\nCase 2: George can compose (Can=true), doesn't want to compose (Want=false). Then, premise 3 doesn't apply because (Can \u2227 Want) is false, so Will could be true or false. But in this case, George might not compose (Will=false). So, \u00acWill is true, and Can is true. Therefore, the statement in question (\u00acWill \u2192 \u00acCan) would be false because the antecedent is true, and the consequent is false.\n\nCase 3: George can't compose (Can=false), wants to compose (Want=true). Then, premise 3 doesn't apply because (Can \u2227 Want) is false, so Will could be true or false. But in this case, George might not compose (Will=false). So, \u00acWill is true, and Can is false. Therefore, the statement in question (\u00acWill \u2192 \u00acCan) would be true because the antecedent is true, and the consequent is true.\n\nCase 4: George can't compose (Can=false), doesn't want to compose (Want=false). Then, premise 3 doesn't apply, so Will could be true or false. But in this case, George might not compose (Will=false). So, \u00acWill is true, and Can is false. Therefore, the statement in question is true.\n\nFrom these cases, in case 2, the statement in question is false. Therefore, the statement is not always true, so it's false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"If George will not compose, George cannot compose\" is false because it's not always true. There's a scenario where George can compose but chooses not to, making the statement false.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
