messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\n"Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show. If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it. Karen does not download "Black Mirror". "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show. If Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it to Lisa.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Karen will share "Black Mirror" to Lisa.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\n\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether Karen will share "Black Mirror" to Lisa based on the given context.Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, the context mentions that "Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show.Then, it says that if a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it.So, from this, I can infer that since "Stranger Things" is popular, Karen binge-watched it.\n\nNext, it states that if and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it.The phrase "if and only if" is important here because it means there\'s a two-way implication.So, Karen downloads a show exactly when she binge-watches it.That means if she binge-watches it, she downloads it, and if she downloads it, she must have binge-watched it.\n\nThen, it says that Karen does not download "Black Mirror."Since "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show, we know it\'s on Netflix.Now, using the previous point, if Karen didn\'t download "Black Mirror," that means she didn\'t binge-watch it either.Despite the claim that Karen will share "Black Mirror" with Lisa if she binge-watches a Netflix show, the fact remains that Karen did not download the show and therefore did not binge-watch it. As a result, she will not share it with Lisa, making the statement that she will share "Black Mirror" false.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
