messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to solve this logical reasoning problem.Let me read through the context and the question carefully..\n\nThe context has several conditional statements, and I need to figure out if Mary needs to earn money for her tuition based on these statements.Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, let\'s list out all the given statements:.\n\n1.If people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition.(Work → Need money).\n2.If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus.(Takeout → Work).\n3.People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes.(Takeout ∨ Enjoy dining hall).\n4.If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters.(Enjoy → Not picky).\n5.If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.(Enjoy → Spend time).\n6.If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.(Mary works and needs → Not picky ∧ Not need money).\n\nWait, that last statement seems a bit confusing.Let me parse it again.It says if Mary works in student jobs and needs to earn money, then she is neither picky nor needs to earn money.That seems contradictory because the conclusion says she doesn\'t need to earn money, but the premise includes that she does.So, this might be a key point.\n\nNow, the question is: Is the statement "Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition" true, false, or uncertain?.\n\nLet me try to map out the relationships.\n\nFrom statement 3, we know that Mary either orders takeout frequently or enjoys dining hall meals.These are the only two options.\n\nIf Mary orders takeout frequently (Takeout), then from statement 2, she works in student jobs (Work).If she works, from statement 1, she needs to earn money (Need money).So, in this scenario, Mary needs money.\n\nAlternatively, if Mary enjoys dining hall meals (Enjoy), then from statement 4, she\'s not picky, and from statement 5, she spends a lot of time there.But what about her need to earn money?The context doesn\'t directly link enjoying dining halls to needing money, except through the other statements.\n\nWait, but statement 6 says that if Mary works and needs money, then she is neither picky nor needs money.That seems like a contradiction because the premise includes needing money, but the conclusion says she doesn\'t.So, this might be a way to show that Mary cannot both work and need money because it leads to a contradiction.\n\nLet me think about this.If Mary works (from Takeout → Work), then she needs money (from Work → Need money).But if she works and needs money, then according to statement 6, she doesn\'t need money.That\'s a contradiction.Therefore, the assumption that Mary works must be false.So, Mary cannot work in student jobs on campus because that would lead to a contradiction.\n\nSince Mary doesn\'t work, from statement 2, if she didn\'t work, then she doesn\'t order takeout frequently (contrapositive: ~Work → ~Takeout).Therefore, Mary must enjoy dining hall meals (from statement 3: Takeout ∨ Enjoy, so if not Takeout, then Enjoy).\n\nIf Mary enjoys dining hall meals, from statement 4, she\'s not picky.But what about her need to earn money?The context doesn\'t directly say that enjoying dining halls means she doesn\'t need money.However, since she doesn\'t work (because working leads to a contradiction), she might not need money.But wait, statement 1 says that if she works, she needs money, but it doesn\'t say anything about not working.So, maybe she doesn\'t need money because she doesn\'t work, but I\'m not sure.\n\nWait, let me re-examine statement 6.It says if Mary works and needs money, then she is neither picky nor needs money.So, if Mary works and needs money, then she doesn\'t need money.That\'s a contradiction, so the only way to avoid it is if Mary doesn\'t work or doesn\'t need money.But if she doesn\'t work, then from statement 2, she doesn\'t order takeout, so she must enjoy dining halls.But does enjoying dining halls affect her need for money?Not directly, unless we can infer that if she doesn\'t work, she doesn\'t need money.But statement 1 only says that working leads to needing money, not the other way around.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m overcomplicating.Let\'s approach it differently.Let\'s assume Mary works.Then, from statement 1, she needs money.But from statement 6, if she works and needs money, then she doesn\'t need money.That\'s a contradiction, so Mary cannot work.Therefore, Mary doesn\'t work.From statement 2, if she doesn\'t work, she doesn\'t order takeout.So, she must enjoy dining halls.But does that mean she doesn\'t need money?Not necessarily, unless we can infer that not working means she doesn\'t need money.But the context doesn\'t say that.It only says that working leads to needing money, but not the converse.\n\nWait, but statement 6 says that if Mary works and needs money, then she doesn\'t need money.So, the only way for Mary to not have a contradiction is if she doesn\'t work or doesn\'t need money.But if she doesn\'t work, then she doesn\'t order takeout, so she enjoys dining halls.But does enjoying dining halls affect her need for money?Not directly.So, maybe she still needs money, but she doesn\'t work.But then, how does she get the money?The context doesn\'t say.So, perhaps she needs money, but she doesn\'t work, which is possible.But statement 1 only says that working leads to needing money, not that needing money leads to working.\n\nWait, but statement 6 is a bit tricky.It says if Mary works and needs money, then she is neither picky nor needs money.So, if Mary works and needs money, then she doesn\'t need money.That\'s a contradiction, so the only way to avoid it is if Mary doesn\'t work or doesn\'t need money.But if she doesn\'t work, then she doesn\'t order takeout, so she enjoys dining halls.But does that mean she doesn\'t need money?Not necessarily.So, perhaps Mary needs money, but she doesn\'t work, which is possible.But the context doesn\'t provide information about other ways she might earn money.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the conclusion is that Mary doesn\'t need money because if she worked, she would need money, but that leads to a contradiction, so she must not need money.But I\'m not sure.\n\nWait, let\'s try to structure this with logical implications.\n\nLet me denote:.\n\nW: Mary works in student jobs on campus.\n\nN: Mary needs to earn money.\n\nMary doesn\'t order takeout frequently.\n\nMary doesn\'t enjoy dining hall meals.\n\nMary isn\'t picky.\n\nFrom the statements:\n\n1.¬W → N.\n\n2.¬T → W.\n\n3.T ∨ E.\n\n4.E → ¬P.\n\n5.E → (spends time eating and catching up).\n\n6.(¬W ∧ ¬N) → (¬P ∧ ¬N).\n\nNow, the question is about N: does Mary need money?\n\nLet\'s assume ¬W is true. Then from 1, N is true. But from 6, (¬W ∧ ¬N) → ¬N, which is a contradiction. Therefore, ¬W must be false.\n\nSo, ¬W is true. Therefore, from 2, W → T (contrapositive). So, T is true.\n\nFrom 3, T ∨ E, and since T is true, E must be false.\n\nFrom E being false, from 4, ¬P is false.\n\nBut what about N? From 1, ¬W → N, but ¬W is false, so N could be either true or false. The statement 1 doesn\'t say anything about N when ¬W is false. So, N could be true or false.\n\nBut wait, statement 6 says that if ¬W and ¬N are both true, then ¬N is true. But since ¬W is false, the antecedent of statement 6 is true, so the implication is false regardless of N. So, statement 6 doesn\'t give us any information about N when ¬W is false.\n\nTherefore, N could be true or false. So, we can\'t determine for sure whether Mary needs money or not. Therefore, the answer would be certain.\n\nWait, but let me think again. If Mary works, does she need money? The context doesn\'t say that working means she needs money. So, she might still need money for tuition, and she works. So, maybe she has other ways to earn money, or perhaps she doesn\'t need it. But the context doesn\'t specify. Therefore, we can\'t determine for sure whether N is true or false.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]  9%|9         | 19/204 [01:41<13:51,  4.49s/it]
