American.\n\nT: Yuri is tall.\n\nLs: Yuri wears large shoes.\n\nLc: Yuri wears large clothes.\n\nGiven:\n\n1. Some B are not A. So, \u2203x (Bx \u2227 \u00acAx). But this doesn't affect Yuri directly.\n\n2. All B are T. So, B \u2192 T.\n\n3. T \u2192 Ls.\n\n4. Ls \u2192 Lc.\n\n5. \u00acLc (Yuri does not wear large clothes).\n\nFrom 4 and 5: \u00acLc \u2192 \u00acLs (contrapositive). So, Yuri does not wear large shoes.\n\nFrom 3 and \u00acLs: \u00acLs \u2192 \u00acT (contrapositive). So, Yuri is not tall.\n\nFrom 2: B \u2192 T. So, if Yuri were a basketball player, Yuri would be tall. But Yuri is not tall, so \u00acB. So, Yuri is not a basketball player.\n\nNow, the statement is: If \u00acLs, then B \u2227 A. But we have \u00acLs, which leads to \u00acB. So, the statement is saying \u00acLs \u2192 (B \u2227 A). But since \u00acLs leads to \u00acB, the conclusion B \u2227 A is false. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nWait, but the statement is: If Yuri does not wear large shoes, then Yuri is an American basketball player. So, the conclusion is that Yuri is an American basketball player. But from the premises, if Yuri doesn't wear large shoes, Yuri isn't a basketball player at all. So, the conclusion is false because Yuri can't be a basketball player if they don't wear large shoes.\n\nTherefore, the statement is false. So, the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The statement is: If Yuri does not wear large shoes, then Yuri is an American basketball player.\n\nBut from the premises, if Yuri doesn't wear large shoes, Yuri isn't a basketball player. So, the conclusion that Yuri is a basketball player is false. Therefore, the entire implication is false because the conclusion is false when the premise is true.\n\nWait, but in logic, an implication is only false when the premise is true and the conclusion is false. So, in this case, the premise is \u00acLs, which is true (since Yuri doesn't wear large shoes), and the conclusion is B \u2227 A, which is false because Yuri isn't a basketball player. Therefore, the implication is false.\n\nSo, the answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is false because if Yuri does not wear large shoes, they cannot be a basketball player, let alone an American one.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
