f John has at least one child, then he does not have a wedding and does not travel to Paris. So, Child \u2192 \u00acWedding \u2227 \u00acParis.\n\nTherefore, if John has a child, he can't have a wedding. But from the first statement, if he has a wedding, he must have a child. So, the only way for John to have a wedding is if he doesn't have a child. But if he doesn't have a child, can he have a wedding? Wait, no, because statement 1 says that having a wedding requires having a child. So, if John doesn't have a child, he can't have a wedding.\n\nTherefore, John cannot have a wedding at all. Because if he has a child, he can't have a wedding, and if he doesn't have a child, he can't have a wedding either. So, John cannot have a wedding.\n\nTherefore, John does not have a wedding. So, he doesn't have a well-attended wedding. Also, since he doesn't have a wedding, from statement 2, he can't travel to Paris because traveling to Paris requires having a wedding.\n\nTherefore, John neither has a well-attended wedding nor travels to Paris. So, the statement is true.\n\nWait, but the last statement in the context says: If John has a wedding that is well-attended, then John has a wedding in July or, if not, then he travels to Paris for his honeymoon.\n\nBut since John can't have a wedding, this statement is irrelevant because the antecedent is false. So, the implication is true regardless.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that John has neither a well-attended wedding nor travels to Paris. So, the statement is true.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't miss anything.\n\nAnother approach: Let's assume John has a well-attended wedding. Then, according to the last statement, either his wedding is in July or he travels to Paris.\n\nBut from earlier, if John has a wedding, he must have a child. But if he has a child, he can't have a wedding. So, this is a contradiction. Therefore, John cannot have a wedding, so he can't have a well-attended wedding. Therefore, the statement that he