      "text": " So, Peter is not a foodie who goes out frequently.\n\nNow, what about the other part: does Peter belong to a household with a high net income?\n\nFrom premise 5, high net income \u2192 busy parents \u2192 only child (from premise 4).But Peter is not an only child, as per our conclusion.So, if Peter is not an only child, then from premise 4, the contrapositive is: if not only child, then not busy parents.So, \u00acOnly child \u2192 \u00acBusy parents.\n\nAnd from premise 5, high net income \u2192 busy parents.So, if Peter's household has a high net income, then they have busy parents, which would mean Peter is an only child.But we've established that Peter is not an only child.Therefore, Peter cannot belong to a household with a high net income.\n\nSo, Peter is neither a foodie who goes out frequently nor does he belong to a household with a high net income.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Peter is either a foodie who goes out frequently or belongs to a household with a high net income\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check.\n\nIf Peter is neither a spiller nor an only child, then:\n\n- He is not a spiller, so he's not a foodie who goes out frequently (since foodies spill).\n\n- He is not an only child, so from premise 4, his parents are not busy (since only children have busy parents).And from premise 5, high net income implies busy parents, so if his parents are not busy, his household doesn't have a high net income.\n\nTherefore, Peter is neither a foodie nor in a high-income household.So, the statement that he is either one or the other is false.\n\nSo, the answer should be B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is false because Peter cannot be a foodie (as he doesn't spill food) nor belong to a high-income household (as he isn't an only child, implying his parents aren't busy, which contradicts high net income).\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
