messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve.Let me try to break it down step by step.The context gives several conditional statements, and I need to figure out whether the given statement about Mary is true, false, or uncertain.\n\nFirst, let me list out all the given premises to make it clearer:.\n\n1.If people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition.(Let\'s call this Premise 1).\n2.If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus.(Premise 2).\n3.People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes.(Premise 3).\n4.If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters.(Premise 4).\n5.If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.(Premise 5).\n6.If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.(Premise 6).\n\nWait, Premise 6 seems a bit confusing.Let me parse it again.It says: If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.Hmm, that seems contradictory because the antecedent includes needing to earn money, and the consequent says she doesn\'t need to earn money.That might be a typo or maybe I\'m misinterpreting it.Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe it\'s saying that if Mary works in student jobs and needs to earn money, then she is neither picky nor does she need to earn money.That would mean that the consequent contradicts the antecedent, which might not make sense.Alternatively, perhaps it\'s a typo, and it should say that she is neither picky nor does she need to earn money.But that would mean that working in student jobs and needing money leads to not needing money, which is a contradiction.Maybe I should proceed and see how it fits into the other premises.\n\nNow, the question is about Mary.The statement to evaluate is: Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n\nSo, the statement is: Mary is not picky OR (if Mary is picky, then she spends a lot of time eating and catching up with friends in the dining halls).\n\nIn logical terms, this can be written as: ¬P ∨ (P → Q), where P is "Mary is picky" and Q is "Mary spends a lot of time eating and catching up with friends in the dining halls.".\n\nBut ¬P ∨ (P → Q) is equivalent to ¬P ∨ (¬P ∨ Q), which simplifies to ¬P ∨ Q.So the statement is equivalent to "Mary is not picky or she spends a lot of time eating and catching up with friends in the dining halls.".\n\nNow, let\'s see what we can deduce about Mary.\n\nFrom Premise 6: If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money, then she is neither picky nor needs to earn money.Wait, that seems contradictory because the antecedent includes needing to earn money, and the consequent says she doesn\'t need to earn money.That would mean that if Mary works in student jobs and needs money, then she doesn\'t need money, which is a contradiction.So perhaps Premise 6 is a typo, and it should say that if Mary works in student jobs and needs to earn money, then she is neither picky nor does she need to earn money.But that would mean that working in student jobs and needing money leads to not needing money, which is impossible.Alternatively, maybe it\'s supposed to say that if Mary works in student jobs and needs to earn money, then she is neither picky nor does she need to earn money.But that would mean that the consequent contradicts the antecedent, which doesn\'t make sense.Maybe I\'m misinterpreting it.\n\nWait, perhaps Premise 6 is correctly stated, and it\'s a way to say that if Mary works in student jobs and needs to earn money, then she is not picky and does not need to earn money.But that would mean that the antecedent includes needing money, and the consequent says she doesn\'t need money, which is a contradiction.So perhaps Premise 6 is a way to say that if Mary works in student jobs and needs to earn money, then she is not picky and doesn\'t need to earn money, which is impossible.Therefore, perhaps Premise 6 is a way to say that if Mary works in student jobs and needs to earn money, then she is not picky and doesn\'t need to earn money, which is a contradiction, meaning that the antecedent cannot be true.Therefore, Mary cannot both work in student jobs and need to earn money.So, either she doesn\'t work in student jobs, or she doesn\'t need to earn money.\n\nBut let\'s proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let\'s consider Premise 1: If someone works in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money for tuition.So, working in student jobs → need money.\n\nPremise 2: If someone orders takeout frequently, then they work in student jobs.So, takeout frequently → work in student jobs.\n\nPremise 3: People either order takeout frequently or enjoy dining hall meals.So, takeout frequently ∨ enjoy dining hall.\n\nPremise 4: If someone enjoys dining hall meals, then they are not picky.So, enjoy dining hall → ¬picky.\n\nPremise 5: If someone enjoys dining hall meals, then they spend a lot of time eating and catching up with friends.So, enjoy dining hall → spend time.\n\nPremise 6: If Mary works in student jobs and needs to earn money, then she is neither picky nor needs to earn money.So, (work ∧ need money) → (¬picky ∧ ¬need money).But this seems contradictory because the antecedent includes needing money, and the consequent says she doesn\'t need money.So, this would mean that if Mary works and needs money, then she doesn\'t need money, which is impossible.Therefore, the only way for this implication to hold is if the antecedent is false.So, Mary cannot both work in student jobs and need to earn money.Therefore, either she doesn\'t work in student jobs, or she doesn\'t need to earn money.\n\nBut let\'s see what else we know.The question is about Mary, so let\'s see what we can deduce about her.\n\nFrom Premise 6, we can infer that Mary does not work in student jobs or does not need to earn money.Because if she did both, it would lead to a contradiction.\n\nBut let\'s see if we can find out more about Mary.Let\'s assume that Mary works in student jobs.Then, from Premise 1, she needs to earn money.But from Premise 6, if she works and needs money, then she doesn\'t need money, which is a contradiction.Therefore, Mary cannot work in student jobs.So, Mary does not work in student jobs.\n\nAlternatively, if Mary does not work in student jobs, then Premise 1 doesn\'t say anything about her needing money.So, she might or might not need money.\n\nBut let\'s see if we can find out more.Let\'s consider Premise 2: If someone orders takeout frequently, then they work in student jobs.Since we\'ve deduced that Mary does not work in student jobs, then the contrapositive of Premise 2 is: If someone does not work in student jobs, then they do not order takeout frequently.So, ¬work → ¬takeout frequently.Since Mary works in student jobs, she orders takeout frequently.. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 56%|#####6    | 115/204 [30:13<06:40,  4.50s/it]
