messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people own a large desktop monitor at their home, then they work regularly and often at home. All people who have a fully stocked and impressive home office are people who own a large desktop monitor at their home. All people who work regularly and often at home spend their free time tidying and organizing the spaces in their homes. All people who work regularly and often at home visit the office supply store regularly. If people spend their free time tidying and organizing the spaces in their home, then they do not go to bed late and wake up every day after noon. Remy either both goes to bed late and wakes up every day after noon and spends their free time tidying and organizing the spaces in their home, or they do not go to bed late and wake up every day after noon nor spend their free time tidying and organizing the spaces in their home.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Remy has a fully stocked and impressive home office and also visits the office supply store regularly.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement about Remy is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me list out all the premises provided:.\n\n1.If people own a large desktop monitor at home, then they work regularly and often at home.\n2.All people who have a fully stocked and impressive home office own a large desktop monitor at home.\n3.All people who work regularly and often at home spend their free time tidying and organizing their homes.\n4.All people who work regularly and often at home visit the office supply store regularly.\n5.If people spend their free time tidying and organizing their homes, then they do not go to bed late and wake up every day after noon.\n6.Remy either both goes to bed late and wakes up every day after noon and spends their free time tidying and organizing their home, or they do not do either.\n\nAlright, so the question is whether Remy has a fully stocked and impressive home office and also visits the office supply store regularly.\n\nLet me try to map this out.\n\nFrom premise 2: Having a fully stocked home office implies owning a large desktop monitor.So, if Remy has a fully stocked home office, then Remy owns a large desktop monitor.\n\nFrom premise 1: Owning a large desktop monitor implies working regularly at home.So, if Remy has a fully stocked home office, then Remy works regularly at home.\n\nFrom premise 3: Working regularly at home implies spending free time tidying and organizing the home.So, if Remy works regularly at home, then Remy spends free time tidying.\n\nFrom premise 4: Working regularly at home implies visiting the office supply store regularly.So, if Remy works regularly at home, then Remy visits the store.\n\nNow, looking at premise 5: Spending free time tidying implies not going to bed late and waking up after noon.So, if Remy spends time tidying, then Remy doesn\'t go to bed late and doesn\'t wake up late.\n\nBut premise 6 says Remy either does both (goes to bed late and wakes up late, and spends time tidying) or does neither.Wait, that seems contradictory because premise 5 says if you tidy, you don\'t go to bed late and wake up late.So, if Remy is tidying, then according to premise 5, Remy doesn\'t go to bed late and doesn\'t wake up late.But premise 6 says Remy either does both or does neither.So, if Remy is tidying, then according to premise 5, Remy doesn\'t do the other two (go to bed late and wake up late).But premise 6 says Remy either does both or neither.So, if Remy is tidying, then Remy must not be doing the other two, which would mean Remy is in the "neither" category.But wait, premise 6 says Remy either does both or neither.So, if Remy is tidying, then according to premise 5, Remy doesn\'t go to bed late and doesn\'t wake up late.So, Remy is in the "neither" category.Therefore, Remy does not go to bed late and does not wake up late, and also spends time tidying.\n\nWait, but premise 6 says Remy either does both (go to bed late and wake up late, and spends time tidying) or does neither.So, if Remy is tidying, then according to premise 5, Remy doesn\'t go to bed late and doesn\'t wake up late.But premise 6 says Remy can\'t do just one; it\'s either both or neither.So, if Remy is tidying, then Remy must not be going to bed late and waking up late, which would mean Remy is in the "neither" category.But that would mean Remy is tidying but not going to bed late and not waking up late.But premise 6 says Remy either does both or neither.So, if Remy is tidying, then Remy must be in the "both" category, but that contradicts premise 5.Alternatively, maybe Remy is in the "neither" category, meaning Remy doesn\'t go to bed late, doesn\'t wake up late, and doesn\'t spend time tidying.But that would mean Remy doesn\'t work at home, which would mean Remy doesn\'t have a fully stocked home office.\nGo ahead, this is becoming clearer. I\'ll stick with the same approach.\n\nIgnore the possibilities for Remy based on premise 6.\n\nPremise 6: Remy either (A) both goes to bed late and wakes up late and spends time tidying, or (B) neither goes to bed late nor wakes up late and doesn\'t spend time tidying.\n\nHowever, from premise 5: If Remy spends time tidying, then Remy does not go to bed late and does not wake up late.So, if Remy is in case A (tidying), then Remy can be going to bed late and waking up late, which supports case A.Therefore, case A is possible because premise 5 confirms it.So, Remy could be in case A: both goes to bed late and wakes up late and spends time tidying.\n\nIf Remy does spend time tidying, then from premise 3, which says that working at home implies tidying, the contrapositive would be that if Remy does tidy, then Remy works at home.So, Remy works at home.\n\nIf Remy works at home, then from premise 1, which says owning a large monitor implies working at home, the contrapositive is that if Remy works at home, then Remy owns a large monitor.So, Remy owns a large monitor.\n\nFrom premise 2, having a fully stocked home office implies owning a large monitor.The contrapositive is that if Remy owns a large monitor, then Remy has a fully stocked home office.So, Remy has a fully stocked home office.\n\nAdditionally, from premise 4, working at home implies visiting the office supply store.Since Remy works at home, Remy visits the store regularly.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Remy has a fully stocked home office and visits the office supply store regularly.Therefore, the statement that Remy doesn\'t have both is true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check.If Remy does work at home, then Remy has a fully stocked home office, and visits the store.So, the statement that Remy doesn\'t have both is true\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 66%|######6   | 135/204 [31:27<01:36,  1.40s/it]
