messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people are twins or triplets, then they spend a lot of time hanging out with and playing with their siblings. If people have siblings who are the same age, then they are twins or triplets. If people complain often about annoying siblings, then they have siblings who are the same age. If people do not live at home regularly, then they do not develop strong bonds with their family members. If people spend a lot of time hanging out with and playing with their siblings, then they often did not live at home regularly. Luke is either both a twin or triplet and develops strong bonds with his family members, or he neither is a twin or triplet nor develops strong bonds with his family members.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Luke complains often about annoying siblings.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "Luke complains often about annoying siblings" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me list out all the given premises to make sure I understand them correctly.\n\n1.If people are twins or triplets, then they spend a lot of time hanging out with and playing with their siblings.\n2.If people have siblings who are the same age, then they are twins or triplets.\n3.If people complain often about annoying siblings, then they have siblings who are the same age.\n4.If people do not live at home regularly, then they do not develop strong bonds with their family members.\n5.If people spend a lot of time hanging out with and playing with their siblings, then they often did not live at home regularly.\n6.Luke is either both a twin or triplet and develops strong bonds with his family members, or he neither is a twin or triplet nor develops strong bonds with his family members.\n\nAlright, so the question is about whether Luke complains often about annoying siblings.Let\'s see how the premises connect.\n\nStarting with premise 6: Luke is either both a twin/triplet and has strong family bonds, or he\'s neither and doesn\'t have strong bonds.So, there are two possibilities for Luke:.\n\n- Option A: He is a twin/triplet and has strong bonds.\n- Option B: He is neither and doesn\'t have strong bonds.\n\nLet me consider each option separately.\n\nFirst, let\'s assume Option A: Luke is a twin/triplet and has strong bonds.\n\nFrom premise 1: If he\'s a twin/triplet, he spends a lot of time with his siblings.So, he does spend a lot of time with them.\n\nFrom premise 5: If he spends a lot of time with his siblings, then he often did not live at home regularly.Wait, that seems a bit confusing.So, spending a lot of time with siblings implies he didn\'t live at home regularly.But premise 4 says if he doesn\'t live at home regularly, he doesn\'t develop strong bonds.However, in this case, Luke does have strong bonds, so that would mean he must live at home regularly.But premise 5 says that if he spends a lot of time with siblings, he didn\'t live at home regularly.So there\'s a contradiction here.\n\nWait, let me re-examine that.Premise 5: If people spend a lot of time with siblings, then they often did not live at home regularly.So, if Luke spends a lot of time with his siblings (because he\'s a twin/triplet), then he often didn\'t live at home regularly.But premise 4 says if you don\'t live at home regularly, you don\'t develop strong bonds.But in this case, Luke does have strong bonds, which would mean he must live at home regularly.Luke does not complain often about annoying siblings, given that he does not have siblings of the same age.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
