r and doesn't have strong bonds. Therefore, he doesn't have same age siblings, so he doesn't complain often.\n\nSo, the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't miss anything.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in assuming that Option A leads to a contradiction. Let me check again.\n\nIf Luke is a twin/triplet, he spends a lot of time with siblings (premise 1). From premise 5, spending a lot of time with siblings implies he often didn't live at home regularly. But premise 4 says if he didn't live at home regularly, he doesn't develop strong bonds. But in Option A, he does develop strong bonds, which contradicts premise 4. Therefore, Option A is impossible, so Luke must be in Option B: neither twin/triplet nor strong bonds.\n\nTherefore, he doesn't have same age siblings, so he doesn't complain often. So the statement is false.\n\nYes, that makes sense.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Luke complains often about annoying siblings\" is false because Luke does not have siblings of the same age, as he is neither a twin nor a triplet. Therefore, he cannot complain often about annoying siblings.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
