messages:   67%|######7   | 137/204 [31:39<03:46,  3.38s/it][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nInternational students in US have either an F1 visa or a J1 visa. An international student in US with an F1 visa needs to apply for CPT or OPT if the student wants to work in the US. Mike is an international student. Mike needs to apply for CPT if he wants to work in the US.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mike has a J1 visa.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "Mike has a J1 visa" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that international students in the US have either an F1 visa or a J1 visa.So, Mike, being an international student, must have one of these two visas.That\'s clear.\n\nNext, it mentions that if a student has an F1 visa, they need to apply for CPT or OPT to work in the US.Mike is an international student who needs to apply for CPT if he wants to work.So, Mike needs CPT, which implies he has an F1 visa because that\'s the visa that requires CPT or OPT for work.\n\nWait, but the question is about whether Mike has a J1 visa.Since Mike needs to apply for CPT, he must have an F1 visa.Therefore, he doesn\'t have a J1 visa.So the statement that Mike has a J1 visa would be true.\n\nI think that\'s the reasoning.Let me make sure I didn\'t miss anything.The context clearly states that F1 visa holders need CPT/OPT to work, and Mike needs CPT, so he must be on an F1 visa.Therefore, he can\'t have a J1 visa\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
